## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request body into memory — that's why it OOMs on large files. Instead, read the multipart body **section by section** using `MultipartReader` and stream each file directly to disk.

### Key changes needed:

1. **Disable form binding & increase request size limits**
2. **Read the raw multipart stream yourself**

```csharp
// Program.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities;
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Raise the max request body size (default is ~28MB)
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpContext context) =>
{
    var contentType = context.Request.ContentType;
    if (contentType is null || !contentType.Contains("multipart/"))
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart request.");

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(
        MediaTypeHeaderValue.Parse(contentType).Boundary).Value;

    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(boundary))
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing boundary.");

    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, context.Request.Body);
    var section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();

    var uploadDir = Path.Combine(app.Environment.ContentRootPath, "Uploads");
    Directory.CreateDirectory(uploadDir);

    while (section is not null)
    {
        var hasContentDisposition = ContentDispositionHeaderValue
            .TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var disposition);

        if (hasContentDisposition && disposition!.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            var fileName = Path.GetRandomFileName(); // avoid path traversal
            var savePath = Path.Combine(uploadDir, fileName);

            // Stream directly to disk — no large memory buffer
            await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
            await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);
        }

        section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();
    }

    return Results.Ok("Upload complete.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery(); // minimal APIs enforce antiforgery on form posts by default
```

### Why this works

| Concern | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Memory usage | `CopyToAsync` streams in small chunks (~80 KB default) |
| Kestrel limit | `MaxRequestBodySize` raised to 2 GB |
| Form model-binding | Bypassed entirely — no `[FromForm]` / `IFormFile` |
| Path traversal | Ignored the client filename; used `GetRandomFileName()` |

### If you're behind IIS / HTTP.sys

Add to `web.config`:

```xml
<system.webServer>
  <security>
    <requestFiltering>
      <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="2147483648" />
    </requestFiltering>
  </security>
</system.webServer>
```

### Optional: disable form value limits globally

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<FormOptions>(opt =>
{
    opt.MultipartBodyLengthLimit = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024;
});
```

This keeps memory flat regardless of file size because only one small buffer is ever in flight at a time.
